How many processes does this create, and how many lines does it print?

```c
int main(void) {
    fork();
    fork();
    fork();
    printf("hello\n");
    return 0;
}
```

Each `fork` doubles the population. Start with one process; after the first `fork` there are 2, after the second 4, after the third 8. So $2^{3} = 8$ processes exist, of which $8 - 1 = 7$ were newly created. Every one of the 8 reaches the `printf`, so **8 lines** are printed.

Now change it:

```c
fork();
fork() && fork();
fork();
```

Work left to right. After the first `fork` there are 2 processes. Each evaluates `fork() && fork()`. In C, `&&` short-circuits: the second `fork()` runs only if the first returned a non-zero value, i.e. only in the parent. So of the 2 processes: each forks, giving a child (returns 0, short-circuits, no further fork) and a parent (non-zero, so it forks again, producing one more process). Each of the 2 original processes therefore becomes 3, giving 6. The final unconditional `fork` doubles this to **12**.

## Hardware primitives

Software-only solutions are delicate, so hardware supplies an atomic read-modify-write instruction.

**Test-and-set:**

```c
boolean TestAndSet(boolean *target) {
    boolean rv = *target;
    *target = true;
    return rv;
}
/* usage */
while (TestAndSet(&lock)) ;   /* spin */
/* critical section */
lock = false;
```

**Swap / compare-and-swap** are equivalent in power. The naive loops above give mutual exclusion and progress but **not bounded waiting**: a process can be unlucky forever. The bounded-waiting version adds a `waiting[]` array and hands the lock explicitly to the next waiter in cyclic order.

Disabling interrupts also gives mutual exclusion on a uniprocessor, but it is useless on a multiprocessor (the other CPUs keep running) and dangerous if the critical section is long.

## Semaphores

A **semaphore** is an integer with two atomic operations. Dijkstra called them $P$ and $V$; the textbook names are `wait` and `signal`.

```
wait(S):   S = S - 1;
           if (S < 0) block the caller on S's queue;

signal(S): S = S + 1;
           if (S <= 0) wake one process from S's queue;
```

The arithmetic gives a very useful invariant: at any moment

$$S = S_{\text{init}} - (\text{completed waits}) + (\text{signals})$$

and when $S$ is negative, $|S|$ is exactly the **number of processes blocked** on it. A **binary semaphore** (mutex) takes values 0 and 1 only; a **counting semaphore** may take any value and is used to count instances of a resource.

Semaphores as defined here block rather than spin, so they do not waste CPU — but the `wait`/`signal` pair themselves must be atomic, which is normally arranged with a short spinlock or by disabling interrupts for a few instructions.

Misuse is easy and is examined: swapping `wait(mutex)` and `signal(mutex)` destroys mutual exclusion; omitting `signal(mutex)` deadlocks everyone; and in the producer-consumer solution, taking `wait(mutex)` **before** `wait(empty)` deadlocks the system because a full-buffer producer sleeps holding the mutex the consumer needs.

## The classic problems

**Bounded buffer (producer-consumer).** Three semaphores: `mutex = 1`, `empty = n`, `full = 0`.

```
Producer:                     Consumer:
  wait(empty);                  wait(full);
  wait(mutex);                  wait(mutex);
  ... insert item ...           ... remove item ...
  signal(mutex);                signal(mutex);
  signal(full);                 signal(empty);
```

The counting semaphores must be acquired **outside** the mutex. `empty` counts free slots, `full` counts filled slots, and `empty + full = n` when nobody is mid-operation.

**Readers-writers.** Many readers may share; a writer needs exclusive access. The first (reader-priority) solution uses `mutex` to protect a `readcount` and a `wrt` semaphore held by the first reader and released by the last; writers can starve. The second (writer-priority) solution starves readers instead. A fair solution needs a queue.

**Dining philosophers.** Five philosophers, five chopsticks, each needs the two beside it. Naively picking up left then right deadlocks when all five pick up their left chopstick at once. Standard fixes: allow at most four philosophers to sit at once; make one philosopher (say the odd-numbered ones) pick up right first, breaking the symmetry and hence the circular wait; or pick up both chopsticks atomically under a mutex.
